similar systems to achieve three-dimensional, basin-scale coverage. Large initiatives such as the Southern Ocean Carbon and Climate Observations and Modeling (SOCCOM) project depend heavily on profiling floats to capture year-round biogeochemical dynamics across wide regions (Sarmiento et al. 2023). When these high-speed mobile platforms traverse sharp spatiotemporal pCO2 gradients (e.g., ocean fronts or eddies), sensor hysteresis produces a pronounced temporal smearing effect that displaces measured values in space and time and can bias carbon flux estimates (Bittig et al. 2014; Mihaylov and El Naggar 2021). Consequently, untethered mobile observation platforms require sensors or correction methods that provide rapid dynamic responses without exceeding tight payloads, volumes, and energy budgets. The observation platform type also strongly affects data quality. Fixed buoys and seabed observatories deliver high-frequency and long-duration records but are spatially constrained (Sutton et al. 2014). Mobile platforms extend spatial coverage, but impose stringent constraints on sensor size, weight, and power. Remotely operated vehicles (ROVs) used to survey seafloor vents and cold seeps can host relatively large, fast pCO2 systems that achieve second-level response times through auxiliary hardware. However, these solutions are often bulky and power-hungry (Zhang et al. 2017; Zhang et al. 2023). In contrast, Argo floats, gliders, and several AUVs must use miniaturized, low-power sensors that trade-off accuracy and dynamic response to meet payload (Roemmich et al. 2009; Rudnick 2016). As hardware miniaturization and response improvements encounter physical constraints, software-based compensation for the sensor dynamic lag has become an attractive and necessary alternative. A complicating factor for software correction efforts is the heterogeneity of marine pCO2 time series. Differences among manufacturers, production batches, deployment ages, and usage histories produce datasets that exhibit heterogeneous time-series (HTS) characteristics, such as large variability in statistical properties (mean and variance) and uneven lengths, which challenge conventional processing and modeling approaches (Fawaz et al. 2019). Physics-based numerical methods such as linear time-invariant (LTI) inverse filtering have been used to reconstruct delayed signals. Although grounded in sensor physics, these methods tend to amplify highfrequency environmental noise when computing concentration gradients, thereby producing severe signal distortions. Recent advances in data-driven and hybrid approaches have offered promising alternatives. Machine learning and intelligent optimization methods have shown superior performance over simple empirical corrections for complex and dynamic environmental signals (Rajwar
et al. 2023; Wang et al. 2024). In the emerging domain of the ‘Smart Ocean’, artificial intelligence has been successfully applied to highly nonlinear oceanographic problems, including monitoring and adaptive exploration (Song et al. 2023). Sequence models such as long shortterm memory (LSTM) networks augmented with attention mechanisms have been used to extract transient features and suppress fluid-dynamic disturbances for platforms such as underwater gliders (Tian et al. 2025). These successes motivated the use of physics-informed deep learning to resolve the trade-off between time-lag correction and noise amplification in mobile in situ pCO2 sensing. In this study, we developed a physics-informed hybrid deep learning framework to overcome the limitations of traditional physical compensation. Our approach combines a single-layer bidirectional LSTM (BiLSTM) with an attention mechanism to capture transient concentration gradients without oversmoothing, and cascades a Kalman filter as a post-processing stage to suppress small-scale prediction jitter without introducing secondary physical lag. The main contributions of this study are as follows.
1) Hybrid correction framework: We propose a method integrating single-layer BiLSTM with attention that balances time-lag compensation and noise suppression, avoiding the high-frequency noise amplification typical of LTI inverse filtering, while correcting the phase delay and minimizing the residual error. 2) Demonstration of attention utility: Ablation experiments showed that the attention mechanism is essential for a shallow recurrent network to recognize rapid gradient changes, reduce delayed responses observed in standard recurrent architectures during sharp pCO2 fluctuations, and enable phase correction without excessive smoothing. 3) Kalman filter integration: Cascading a Kalman filter as a postprocessing module smoothens the highfrequency prediction variability from the neural network. Quantitatively, the combined method reduced the system response time by 72.8% for typical rising sequences and 43.5% for falling sequences while lowering the root mean square error (RMSE) by 12%28%.
Combined, these results indicate that a physicsinformed hybrid learning approach can substantially improve the dynamic fidelity of pCO2 measurements using miniaturized sensors on mobile oceanographic platforms, thereby enabling more accurate estimates of ocean carbon fluxes under realistic deployment constraints.


