2 Related work
2.1 Time series analysis and the evolution of deep learning model
Time-series analysis remains a fundamental challenge in the scientific and engineering domains. Traditional methods, such as the autoregressive integrated moving average (ARIMA), often struggle to effectively capture the nonlinear and non-stationary characteristics typical of marine environments. Consequently, deep learning approaches have become increasingly prominent in this field (Lim and Zohren 2021). To address the vanishing gradient problem in conventional recurrent neural networks (RNNs), advanced architectures, including long short-term memory (LSTM) networks (Sherstinsky 2020) and gated recurrent units (GRUs), introduce gating mechanisms that enable the effective modeling of long-term dependencies (Fawaz et al. 2019; Hewage et al. 2021). Furthermore, bidirectional LSTM (BiLSTM) enhances contextual representation by incorporating information from both the past and future directions, thereby overcoming the limitations of unidirectional processing (Sagheer and Kotb 2019; Lin et al. 2023). The integration of attention mechanisms further improves the model performance by allowing a dynamic focus on critical historical information within sequences (Niu et al. 2021). Consequently, hybrid BiLSTM-attention architectures have demonstrated a strong capability to model complex and highly variable sequences (Xiang et al. 2020; Zhu et al. 2022). Recent advances in time-series modeling and data augmentation techniques (e.g., Informer and PatchTST) have achieved state-of-the-art performance in long-sequence modeling (Xiang et al. 2020; Zhu et al. 2022). However, these models are typically heavily parameterized and require large-scale datasets for effective training to avoid overfitting. In marine sensor calibration scenarios, where data collection is costly and limited, acquiring large datasets is impractical. Therefore, the BiLSTM-attention framework offers a favorable balance between computational efficiency and predictive accuracy, particularly for small- to medium-sized datasets.
2.2 Methods for correcting sensor response lag
Sensor response lag presents a critical challenge for dynamic measurements. Due to physical constraints on hardware miniaturization, algorithmic ‘soft correction’ methods based on mathematical modeling have become the dominant approach. Classical correction techniques typically focus on the physical response mechanisms of sensors using differential equations or deconvolution algorithms for inverse compensation. For example, LTI system models are commonly employed to perform inverse filtering of aquatic oxygen and carbon dioxide
sensor data (Fiedler et al. 2013; Bittig et al. 2014; Gordon et al. 2020). However, inverse filtering is highly sensitive to high frequency noise. The differentiation of fluctuating signals inherently amplifies noise, often resulting in distorted and oscillatory outputs. To mitigate these effects, alternative approaches such as autoregressive with exogenous input (ARX) models and Kalman filters have been explored (Tomczyk et al. 2022). Although these traditional methods can partially suppress noise, they often fail to achieve instantaneous phase correction simultaneously and rely heavily on accurately specified physical model parameters. Consequently, the fundamental trade-off between ‘time-lag elimination’ and ‘noise amplification’ remains unresolved. This limitation has motivated the adoption of physics-informed data-driven deep learning approaches.
2.3 Attention mechanism and post‐processing micro‐jitter suppression
In real-world marine environments, calibration data collected under varying experimental conditions exhibit significant heterogeneity in noise characteristics and distribution (Zhuang et al. 2021). Advanced approaches, such as transfer learning (Weber et al. 2021; Zhuang et al. 2021), domain adaptation (Wilson and Cook 2020), and meta-learning (Wang et al. 2020) have been widely proposed to address such heterogeneity. However, these methods typically require large datasets to achieve effective domain alignment. Given the high cost and strict control requirements for marine sensor calibration, acquiring such datasets is challenging. Instead of relying on data-intensive approaches, this study directly addresses the core physical challenges of sensor lag and fluid-induced noise through a combination of attention mechanisms and Kalman filtering. When sensors mounted on mobile platforms traverse regions with sharp pCO2 gradients, standard recurrent models often fail to distinguish true concentration changes from background noise, leading to delayed correction. The attention mechanism serves as a dynamic feature selection module, enabling BiLSTM to assign greater importance to salient transition points while suppressing irrelevant or noisy historical information. Although the attention-enhanced model effectively corrects the macroscopic phase lag, its nonlinear nature may introduce high-frequency micro-jitters into the predictions. To ensure the data quality required for marine carbon flux estimation, a Kalman filter was applied as a postprocessing step. As an optimal recursive smoother, it suppresses stochastic fluctuations without introducing additional lags. In addition, before training, each stepresponse sequence was temporally aligned to remove


