3 Methodology
3.1 Experimental data acquisition and heterogeneity construction
The dataset employed in this study consisted of 13 independent marine CO2 time-series sequences collected using a low-cost in situ pCO2 sensor based on membrane inlet and non-dispersive infrared (NDIR) principles (Li et al. 2022), with a sampling frequency of 1 Hz. The experimental setup is shown in Fig. 1. A water-pumped head was positioned at front of the sensor (i.e., the permeable membrane side) and connected to a water pump and a tee valve through tubing. Tap water and seawater, which represent distinct pCO2 levels, were used as the input sources. During the experiments, a tee valve was used to rapidly switch the inlet lines, artificially triggering sharp changes in the concentration step. This design simulates the sharp pCO2 concentration gradients frequently encountered by mobile observation platforms (e.g., underwater gliders and AUVs) as they traverse distinct ocean water masses. The recorded sequences included complete dynamic response cycles, that is, rising, falling, and steady-state phases, providing a rigorous and comprehensive basis for evaluating the lag correction capabilities and noise robustness of the models. For supervised training, the target labels (i.e., ideal ground truth) must accurately reflect the true external physical processes. The ground truth is not an arbitrarily defined instantaneous square wave but a physically derived reference signal. Although water source switching at the valve is nearly instantaneous (< 1 s), the actual pCO2 concentration inside the sensor’s external water chamber changes continuously owing to fluid dynamics. The theoretical transition time is governed by the ratio
of dead volume to pump flow rate. Based on our experimental hardware parameters, the complete fluid replacement process required approximately 10 s. Accordingly, the ground truth was modeled as a continuous logistic step curve with approximately 10 s transition period. The model was trained to map raw sensor signals, whose equilibration exceeded 100 s owing to membrane permeation, to this physically realistic baseline. In practical applications, marine in situ sensors require field calibration to ensure measurement accuracy. However, because of the limited time available for field deployment, the acquired calibration datasets were inherently constrained in size. This operational constraint necessitates a data-efficient modeling approach capable of robust performance with limited training samples. To authentically reflect real-world operational constraints, we constructed a compact laboratory dataset comprising 13 sequences.
3.2 Data preprocessing and sliding‐window segmentation To address the sensor’s physical response lag and suppress high-frequency fluid noise, we propose a hybrid BiLSTM-attention and Kalman filter framework. The overall architecture is shown in Fig. 2. Prior to training the deep learning model, a standardized data preprocessing pipeline was applied to ensure training stability and enhance feature extraction. In practical deployments, marine in situ sensors require field calibration; however, the calibration time is often limited, resulting in small datasets. In this study, 13 independent step-response sequences were collected from the laboratory experiments. For supervised learning, 11 sequences were used for training, whereas two sequences were reserved as independent out-of-distribution test sets: one exhibiting a strictly decreasing trend (Set A) and the other a strictly increasing trend (Set B). These challenging cases were intentionally selected to
