An optimized single-layer BiLSTM-attention architecture was constructed based on the preprocessed dynamic phase data. To simulate continuous real-time monitoring, the input is defined as a sliding window tensor of historical CO2 observations (Fig. 3). The proposed architecture processes the input sliding window through three main components: 1) a single BiLSTM layer for bidirectional temporal feature extraction, 2) an attention layer that dynamically assigns higher connection weights to time steps with significant concentration changes, and 3) a fully connected module that maps the extracted features to the target concentration. To mitigate overfitting under limited data conditions, L2 regularization (coefficient = 0.005) and dropout (rate = 0.5) were applied.
3.4 Experimental setup and evaluation strategy
Model evaluation followed a hold-out validation scheme with offline training and online inference. During training, the 11 sequences were segmented into sliding windows to optimize the network weights. The model weights were fixed during testing. The model is then applied to independent test Sets (A and B) to simulate real-time inference in continuous data streams.
