4 Experimental results and analysis
4.1 Baseline comparison: deep learning vs. traditional methods
To evaluate the phase correction performance and noise robustness of the BiLSTM-attention framework, we conducted comparative experiments on two independent test sets: test Set A (falling trend) and test Set B (rising trend). The proposed framework is benchmarked against three representative baselines.
1) Linear time-invariant (LTI) model: a classical physics-based method for gradient inversion. 2) Auto regressive with exogenous input (ARX) model: which is a traditional linear autoregressive filtering approach. 3) Support vector regression (SVR): a conventional machine learning model lacking temporal memory.
Figure 4a and b show the prediction results for test Sets A and B, respectively, comparing the proposed framework with the LTI, ARX, and SVR models. Figure 4c and d present the corresponding residual error distributions. The ideal ground truth represents the theoretical concentration step change induced by the fluid-switching process described in Section 3.1. As summarized in Table 1 and Fig. 4, the traditional baselines have notable limitations. Although the LTI model reduces the phase delay, its gradient-based formulation amplifies the high-frequency fluid noise, resulting in oscillatory residuals. The ARX and SVR models attenuate this noise, but fail to capture the nonlinear membrane permeation process, leading to a systematic bias and a rightward phase shift during transient dynamics. In contrast, the BiLSTM-attention framework closely tracks the ground truth, achieving R2 > 0.99 and maintaining an RMSE below 2.0 μatm across both test sets. By adaptively weighting the salient features, the model effectively aligns the phase while preserving signal smoothness, thereby balancing lag correction and noise suppression.
4.2 Ablation study: crucial role of the attention mechanism
An ablation study was conducted to assess the contribution of the attention mechanism by comparing the proposed framework with a basic BiLSTM model. As illustrated by the transient response curves (Fig. 5), the model without the attention layer exhibited a pronounced delay during sharp concentration transitions. indicating that without adaptive weighting of the gradient changes, the base recurrent network suffers from a phase lag. Quantitative results further substantiate this observation. In test Set A (falling trend), removing the attention mechanism increases the RMSE from 1.87 μatm


