4.3 Downstream micro‐jitter suppression via KF
While the BiLSTM-attention model effectively corrects macroscopic phase distortions, it introduces minor highfrequency stochastic micro-jitters in the prediction signals. To satisfy the data quality requirements for marine carbon flux estimation, a (KF was employed as a downstream post-processing step. This is shown in Fig. 6a and b for test Sets A and B, respectively. The zoomed-in insets highlight that the cascaded KF enhances signal stability while preserving the corrected transient phase. The KF stage substantially suppressed the high-frequency micro-jitters, achieving a noise reduction of over 50% without degrading the phase alignment (Table 2, Fig. 6). In addition, this post-processing step significantly improved the dynamic response of the system, as quantified by t63 (the time required to reach 63.2% of the steadystate value). The response delay is reduced from 147.3 s to 18.2 s in the rising scenario (an 87.6% reduction), and from 273.3 s to 24.5 s in the falling scenario (a 91.0% reduction). Overall, this hybrid architecture, which combines deep learning-based phase correction with Kalman filtering, effectively resolves the trade-off between hysteresis compensation and noise amplification, enabling highly accurate continuous pCO2 measurements.
4.4 Discussion
Acquiring large, high-quality, in situ marine datasets is both experimentally demanding and costly. This study demonstrates that a data-efficient modeling strategy can achieve accurate dynamic corrections. By extracting the transient phase features and incorporating regularization (L2 and dropout) within a lightweight single-layer
architecture, the model mitigates overfitting and achieves robust generalization (R2 and RMSE) on a limited dataset. This suggests that the proposed neural network is wellsuited for resource-constrained marine sensing applications and requires substantially less training data than highly parameterized architectures such as transformers. While Section 4 highlighted the limitations of the physical LTI and linear ARX models, the present ablation study clarifies the internal contribution of the proposed architecture. Conventional machine learning approaches such as SVR lack the temporal memory required to capture complex fluid dynamics. Although standard BiLSTM can model global temporal trends, it still exhibits a noticeable delay, often exceeding 40 s in our experiments during abrupt pCO2 concentration changes. The attention mechanism alleviates this limitation by selectively weighting transient features, thereby enabling the network to focus on rapid gradient variations. This targeted extraction effectively reduced the residual hysteresis, which is typical of standard recurrent networks. Despite these promising results, the relatively small dataset (13 sequences) introduced inherent limitations. The model may partially overfit laboratory-specific noise characteristics rather than fully capturing universal permeation dynamics. In addition, the sensor response kinetics are intrinsically coupled with environmental variables such as temperature, salinity, and flow rate. Although the current framework has been validated under controlled conditions, its robustness under highly variable or extreme environmental regimes remains to be established. Therefore, future work should focus on expanding the multiparameter
