5 Conclusions
To address sensor response hysteresis and high-frequency fluid noise in continuous marine CO2 monitoring, this study proposes a correction framework that integrates a BiLSTM-attention network with Kalman filtering. The main findings of this study are as follows.
First, the proposed framework mitigates the noise amplification typically observed in LTI-based inverse filtering. It accurately reconstructs phase dynamics on independent test sets with high R2 and low RMSE, and recovers the dynamic pCO2 signal with minimal phase distortion. Second, the ablation results confirm that the attention mechanism is essential. Emphasizing transient features reduces the response delay observed in standard recurrent networks during abrupt concentration step changes. Finally, coupling the neural network with a downstream Kalman filter effectively suppresses highfrequency prediction noise. This cascaded approach
reduces the signal variance by more than 50% and shortens the system response time (t63) by 91.0% and 87.6% for the rising and falling pCO2 scenarios, respectively.
This paper presents a data-efficient deep learning solution for marine sensor calibration. By integrating temporal modeling with adaptive feature weighting and statistical filtering, the proposed framework enhances temporal resolution and data fidelity for in situ marine biogeochemical observations.
